---
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import { type SupportedLanguage } from '../utils/i18n';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import NavBar from '../components/NavBar.astro';

const lang = (Astro.currentLocale as SupportedLanguage) || 'en';
---

<Layout lang={lang} 
  title="How to Calculate Subnet Mask & CIDR | Complete CCNA/CCNP Guide | DevSubnet"
  description="Learn how to calculate subnet masks, determine usable host ranges, and master the Magic Number method for CCNA/CCNP exams. Step-by-step formula derivation with examples."
>
  <div class="app-shell">
    <NavBar lang={lang} path='guide' />

    <main class="page-main">
      <div class="content-container">
        <!-- Breadcrumb -->
        <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
          <a href="/">Home</a> <span class="sep">/</span> <strong>How to Calculate a Subnet</strong>
        </nav>

        <section class="page-header">
          <div class="eyebrow">STEP-BY-STEP EXAM GUIDE</div>
          <h1>How to Calculate a Subnet</h1>
          <p class="lede">
            Subnetting is dividing an IP network into smaller, logical blocks. Learn the binary mechanics, the Magic Number shortcut method used on Cisco CCNA/CCNP exams, and a repeatable paper-math process that works for any IPv4 prefix.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="page-body">
          <div class="text-block">
            <h2>What Is a Subnet Mask?</h2>
            <p>
              A subnet mask is a 32-bit number that separates an IP address into the <strong>network portion</strong> (first bits, marked with binary 1s) and the <strong>host portion</strong> (remaining bits, marked with 0s). The mask tells routers how much of the address identifies the network and how much identifies a device on it. In CIDR notation, the same information is written as a slash followed by the prefix length: a mask of <code>255.255.255.0</code> is written <code>/24</code>.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="text-block">
            <h2>The Core Mathematical Formulas</h2>
            <p>
              Every subnetting calculation comes down to two essential formulas:
            </p>
            <ul class="step-list">
              <li><strong>Total Addresses in Block:</strong> <code>2<sup>h</sup></code>, where <code>h</code> is the number of host bits (<code>32 &minus; prefix length</code>).</li>
              <li><strong>Usable Host Capacity:</strong> <code>2<sup>h</sup> &minus; 2</code>. Two addresses are reserved: the Network Address (first IP, all host bits 0) and the Broadcast Address (last IP, all host bits 1).</li>
              <li><strong>Subnets Created:</strong> <code>2<sup>s</sup></code>, where <code>s</code> is the number of borrowed subnet bits from the classful default boundary.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Example: A <code>/26</code> subnet has 6 host bits, so it holds 2<sup>6</sup> = 64 total addresses and 64 &minus; 2 = <strong>62 usable hosts</strong>.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="text-block">
            <h2>Standard 5-Step Calculation Process</h2>
            <ol class="ordered-list">
              <li>
                <strong>Write the address and mask in binary:</strong> Convert each octet to 8 bits. For <code>192.168.1.130/26</code>, the network bits are the first 26 bits and the remaining 6 bits are host bits.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Find the network address via Bitwise AND:</strong> AND the IP with the mask (1 AND 1 = 1, 0 AND anything = 0). The result is the network address: <code>192.168.1.128</code>.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Find the broadcast address:</strong> Keep the 26 network bits unchanged and set all 6 host bits to 1. This produces <code>192.168.1.191</code>.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Identify the usable host range:</strong> Every address between network and broadcast is usable: <code>192.168.1.129</code> through <code>192.168.1.190</code>.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>State the result in CIDR notation:</strong> Base network address, slash, prefix length: <code>192.168.1.128/26</code>.
              </li>
            </ol>
          </div>

          <div class="text-block">
            <h2>The Magic Number Method (Cisco CCNA & CCNP Fast Paper Math)</h2>
            <p>
              In Cisco CCNA and CCNP certification exams, calculators are prohibited. Network engineers and exam candidates use the <strong>Magic Number method</strong> (also called the <strong>Block Size method</strong>) to calculate subnet IDs, broadcast addresses, and host ranges mentally in under 15 seconds without converting whole 32-bit addresses into binary strings.
            </p>
            <ol class="ordered-list">
              <li>
                <strong>Identify the "Interesting Octet":</strong> Look at the subnet mask to find the octet that is neither <code>255</code> nor <code>0</code>. For example, in <code>255.255.255.192</code> (a <code>/26</code>), the 4th octet is the interesting octet.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Subtract from 256:</strong> Calculate <code>256 &minus; interesting octet = Magic Number</code>. For <code>255.255.255.192</code>, the magic number is <code>256 &minus; 192 = 64</code>. This is your subnet block hop size.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Hop in Multiples of the Magic Number:</strong> Subnet boundaries always start at multiples of the magic number: <code>0, 64, 128, 192, 256...</code>
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Match Your IP Address:</strong> Find where the given IP octet lands. If your host is <code>192.168.1.130/26</code>, the 4th octet is <code>130</code>. Since <code>130</code> falls between <code>128</code> and <code>192</code>, your subnet <strong>Network ID is 192.168.1.128</strong>.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Find Broadcast and Usable Range:</strong> The next subnet begins at <code>192.168.1.192</code>, so the broadcast address is one less: <code>192.168.1.191</code>. Usable host IPs span from <code>192.168.1.129</code> through <code>192.168.1.190</code>.
              </li>
            </ol>
          </div>

          <div class="text-block">
            <h2>Reverse Netmask & Wildcard Mask Conversion</h2>
            <p>
              Standard CIDR notation (like <code>/28</code>) is standard in cloud VPC and modern Linux environments, but Cisco IOS ACLs, OSPF configurations, and legacy firewalls frequently require <strong>dotted-decimal netmasks</strong> or <strong>wildcard masks</strong>.
            </p>
            <ul class="step-list">
              <li><strong>Converting Netmask to CIDR:</strong> Count the total number of binary 1 bits across all 4 octets. For example, <code>255.255.255.240</code> has 8 + 8 + 8 + 4 = 28 ones, which equals <code>/28</code>.</li>
              <li><strong>Calculating Wildcard Masks:</strong> Subtract each octet of the subnet mask from <code>255.255.255.255</code>. For a <code>/28</code> (<code>255.255.255.240</code>), the wildcard mask is <code>255.255.255.255 &minus; 255.255.255.240 = 0.0.0.15</code>.</li>
              <li><strong>Cisco ACL Shortcut:</strong> In Cisco Access Control Lists, a wildcard mask tells the router which bit positions must match (0 = must match, 1 = ignore). DevSubnet automatically generates both Cisco IOS interface configs and wildcard ACL syntax in 1 click.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="text-block">
            <h2>Common CIDR Blocks Cheat Table</h2>
            <div class="guide-table-wrapper">
              <table class="guide-table">
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Prefix</th><th>Subnet Mask</th><th>Wildcard Mask</th><th>Total IPs</th><th>Usable Hosts</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>/32</td><td>255.255.255.255</td><td>0.0.0.0</td><td>1</td><td>1 (Host route)</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>/31</td><td>255.255.255.254</td><td>0.0.0.1</td><td>2</td><td>2 (RFC 3021 P2P)</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>/30</td><td>255.255.255.252</td><td>0.0.0.3</td><td>4</td><td>2 (point-to-point)</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>/29</td><td>255.255.255.248</td><td>0.0.0.7</td><td>8</td><td>6</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>/28</td><td>255.255.255.240</td><td>0.0.0.15</td><td>16</td><td>14</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>/27</td><td>255.255.255.224</td><td>0.0.0.31</td><td>32</td><td>30</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>/26</td><td>255.255.255.192</td><td>0.0.0.63</td><td>64</td><td>62</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>/25</td><td>255.255.255.128</td><td>0.0.0.127</td><td>128</td><td>126</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>/24</td><td>255.255.255.0</td><td>0.0.0.255</td><td>256</td><td>254</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>/23</td><td>255.255.254.0</td><td>0.0.1.255</td><td>512</td><td>510</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>/22</td><td>255.255.252.0</td><td>0.0.3.255</td><td>1,024</td><td>1,022</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>/16</td><td>255.255.0.0</td><td>0.0.255.255</td><td>65,536</td><td>65,534</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>/8</td><td>255.0.0.0</td><td>0.255.255.255</td><td>16,777,216</td><td>16,777,214</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="tips-box">
            <h3>Quick Exam Rule of Thumb</h3>
            <p>
              Need a subnet for N hosts? Double N to cover the two subtracted boundary addresses, round up to the next power of two, count the host bits, and subtract from 32. For example, 50 hosts &rarr; ~50 + 2 = 52 &rarr; next power of two is 64 (2<sup>6</sup>) &rarr; 6 host bits &rarr; 32 &minus; 6 = <code>/26</code>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <div class="try-tool" style="margin-top: 2.5rem; text-align: center;">
          <a href="/" class="btn-return">Test in DevSubnet with Step-by-Step Exam Mode &rarr;</a>
        </div>

        <div class="page-cta" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
          <a href="/" class="btn-return">← Return to Calculator</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </main>

    <Footer lang={lang} />
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .page-main { flex: 1; padding: 3rem 1.5rem 5rem; }
  .content-container { max-width: 54rem; margin: 0 auto; text-align: left; }

  .breadcrumb { font-size: 0.82rem; color: var(--text-secondary); margin-bottom: 2rem; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.4rem; }
  .breadcrumb a { color: var(--accent); text-decoration: none; }
  .breadcrumb a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
  .breadcrumb .sep { color: var(--text-secondary); }

  .page-header { margin-bottom: 3rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-color); padding-bottom: 2.5rem; }
  .page-header .eyebrow { font-size: 0.72rem; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.12em; color: var(--accent-network); text-transform: uppercase; margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
  .page-header h1 { font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3rem); font-weight: 700; color: var(--text-primary); letter-spacing: -0.03em; margin: 0 0 1rem; line-height: 1.1; }
  .page-header .lede { font-size: 1.1rem; color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.65; max-width: 42rem; margin: 0; }

  .page-body { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 3rem; }
  .text-block h2 { font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 600; margin: 0 0 1rem; color: var(--text-primary); }
  .text-block p { font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--text-secondary); margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  .text-block p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .text-block code { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.85rem; background: var(--bg-secondary); padding: 0.1em 0.4em; border-radius: 4px; color: var(--accent-highlight); }

  .step-list, .ordered-list { margin: 0 0 1rem; padding-left: 1.5rem; }
  .ordered-list li, .step-list li { font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--text-secondary); margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }

  .guide-table-wrapper { overflow-x: auto; border: 1px solid var(--border-color); border-radius: var(--radius-md); background: var(--bg-surface); }
  .guide-table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 0.88rem; text-align: left; }
  .guide-table th { background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.03); padding: 0.85rem 1.2rem; font-weight: 600; color: var(--text-primary); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-color); }
  .guide-table td { padding: 0.75rem 1.2rem; color: var(--text-secondary); border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.04); }

  .tips-box { background: var(--bg-surface); border: 1px solid var(--border-color); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-network); border-radius: var(--radius-md); padding: 1.5rem 2rem; }
  .tips-box h3 { font-size: 1.1rem; font-weight: 600; margin: 0 0 0.75rem; color: var(--text-primary); }
  .tips-box p { font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--text-secondary); margin: 0; }

  .page-cta { margin-top: 3rem; }
  .btn-return { display: inline-block; background: var(--text-primary); color: var(--bg-primary); padding: 0.75rem 1.5rem; border-radius: var(--radius-full); font-weight: 600; font-size: 0.875rem; text-decoration: none; transition: opacity var(--transition-fast); }
  .btn-return:hover { opacity: 0.85; }
</style>